/* SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause-Patent
 *
 * The two AW88261 "Music" register profiles of the K60, transcribed from the
 * phone's own ACF tuning file.
 *
 * Where the numbers come from. The blob lives on the device at
 * /vendor/firmware/aw882xx_acf.bin (3,440 bytes, sha256
 * ce0e16d22380565114f56e6a02e4f85959160baccc6c163df6ecaf9db20cbf9e) and is not stored here: .gitignore
 * excludes *.bin and the file is Awinic/Xiaomi tuning data, not ours. What the kernel needs at boot is the register pairs,
 * so this header carries them, generated by
 * E:/k60-work/scratch/audio-work/gen-profile-header.py from that exact blob:
 *
 *   dev_index 0, profile 0 (Music), kind 4: offset 0x3f4, 292 bytes, 57 pairs
 *   dev_index 1, profile 0 (Music), kind 4: offset 0x944, 292 bytes, 57 pairs
 *
 * Each block's own checksum was verified before it was read. Do not edit these
 * arrays: the host test (drivers/PongAudio/tests) compares them against the
 * blob whenever PONG_ACF_FILE points at it, so a hand edit fails the test
 * instead of silently changing what the amplifiers are tuned to.
 *
 * WHICH TABLE BELONGS TO WHICH AMPLIFIER. Not the probe's numbering. In the
 * ACF, dev_index 0 is the part on I2C 0x35 and dev_index 1 the part on 0x34
 * (docs/research/audio/AMPS.md section 8.5, H: hakios.speaker.c:430-431),
 * while this probe calls 0x34 "amp0". Use pong_amp_profile_for_addr(), never
 * the probe index. Loading the other half's table is a calibration mismatch,
 * not a crash: the speaker still plays, just not to its own tuning.
 *
 * The two tables differ in 5 registers (0x05, 0x06, 0x07, 0x08, 0x0f).
 * Everything else is identical, which is exactly why a single shared table
 * looks plausible and is wrong.
 */
